x87 fast path: host FPU for the common case, bit-exact, softfloat otherwise.

Every x87 instruction is a helper call into 80-bit softfloat (128-bit
parts, precision control applied at rounding): hundreds of host
instructions per fadd/fmul. Windows runs the FPU at 53-bit precision
(process default) or 24-bit (Direct3D), so the guest is doing double/float
math with a wider exponent range. When both operands are exact host
doubles (floats), rounding is nearest-even and the result is a normal
number well inside the exponent range, the host's correctly rounded result
equals the 80-bit result rounded to 53 (24) bits; the inexact flag comes
from TwoSum/FMA residuals. Everything else falls back, so results never
change. Covers fadd/fsub/fmul/fdiv (all forms), fsqrt, frndint, fcom*,
fld/fst m32/m64, fild, fist/fistt, and the double<->x80 conversions around
the host-libm transcendentals. -cpu ...,x87-fast=off disables it.

Verified: tools/x87-fast-test.c (host oracle vs. the real x87, millions of
operands incl. edge cases, 0 mismatches) and tools/x87-guest-test.py (DOS
program under TCG, 125k results identical with the fast path on/off;
2.2x on a 7-op double loop on x86-64, Super PI on the M1 Air is the
real-world number). Known softfloat deviation kept on purpose: at 24-bit
precision softfloat rounds double loads and transcendental results to 24
bits (real hardware does not); the fast path defers to softfloat there.

